{# ---------- 1. THE PROBLEM ---------- #}
<section class="section section--soft" id="problem">
  <div class="shell">
    <div class="section-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">The problem</p>
      <h2>Every call is the same call.</h2>
      <p>
        Ask anyone who runs a ward what the nurse call actually tells them, and the answer is
        always the same: a room number and a noise. Everything that would let the team respond
        well — what is wrong, how urgent it is, who is qualified to deal with it, what to bring —
        is missing, and has to be discovered by walking there.
      </p>
    </div>

    <blockquote class="pull" style="margin-bottom: var(--gap)">
      <p>
        “A light goes on. Someone stops what they are doing, walks the length of the corridor,
        finds out it is a glass of water — or finds out it is something they are not qualified to
        handle and has to walk back for a colleague. We do that several hundred times a day and we
        learn nothing from any of it.”
      </p>
      <footer>How ward leadership describes an ordinary shift. Composite, not a single attributed quote.</footer>
    </blockquote>

    <div class="grid grid--3">
      <article class="card">
        <span class="card__index">01</span>
        <h3>The wrong person goes</h3>
        <p>
          Because nobody knows what the call is about, whoever is free responds. Sometimes that is
          someone overqualified doing a task anyone could do. Sometimes it is someone who now has
          to find a colleague with the right qualification, and the patient waits twice.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <span class="card__index">02</span>
        <h3>The room gets left again</h3>
        <p>
          Without knowing the request, staff arrive empty-handed. The equipment or the medication
          is at the other end of the ward, so the visit turns into a round trip and the walking
          gets counted twice.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <span class="card__index">03</span>
        <h3>The interruption lands anyway</h3>
        <p>
          Calls arrive in the middle of drawing up medication or working on a line — tasks where an
          interruption is a safety risk, not an inconvenience. An accidental press still has to be
          checked in person, because nothing distinguishes it from an emergency.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <p style="margin-top: 2rem">
      <a href="/en/challenge/">Read the long version of the problem &rarr;</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ---------- 2. THE SOLUTION ---------- #}
<section class="section" id="solution">
  <div class="shell">
    <div class="section-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">What PATON does</p>
      <h2>Give the call a subject line.</h2>
      <p>
        PATON is a smart nurse-call system. It keeps the thing that works about the old bell — a
        patient can always reach someone, instantly — and adds the one thing it never had: what the
        call is actually about.
      </p>
    </div>

    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        <h3>The patient asks for something specific</h3>
        <p>
          A device at the bed replaces the single button with a short list of the things patients
          actually ask for, plus one unmistakable emergency action that always goes out at top
          priority.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>The request reaches the right device</h3>
        <p>
          Staff on shift carry a nurse device. Each request arrives as a readable alert — room, bed,
          what is needed, how long it has been waiting — rather than a room number and a chime.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>The ward sees it in order</h3>
        <p>
          Alerts are ordered by urgency and matched to the qualification the request needs, so an
          emergency is never sitting behind a request for a blanket.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Every call leaves a record</h3>
        <p>
          Because each request has a type, a time and an outcome, a ward can finally see its own
          call pattern — which is the part no bell has ever been able to give anyone.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <div class="button-row" style="margin-top: 2.5rem">
      <a class="button" href="/en/solution/">The system in detail</a>
      <a class="button button--ghost" href="/en/case-study/">Where it has run</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

